" (https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/a9Proc23/a9Proc23.pdf)\nciteturn9909view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/a9Proc23/a9Proc23.pdf\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 1001\nL0@P0: #A9 INTEGERS 24A (2024)\nL1@P0: HENRIK ERIKSSON’S BULGARIAN SOLITAIRE VARIANT\nL2@P0: Brian Hopkins\nL3@P0: Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Saint Peter’s University, Jersey City,\nL4@P0: New Jersey\nL5@P0: bhopkins@saintpeters.edu\nL6@P0: Received: 9/3/23, Accepted: 4/5/24, Published: 5/27/24\nL7@P0: Abstract\nL8@P0: Bulgarian Solitaire, originally a recreational mathematics topic about pile sizes of\nL9@P0: various objects under a rearrangement procedure, is now studied as an operation\nL10@P0: on the integer partitions of a fixed number. Martin Gardner popularized the math\u0002ematical puzzle in one of his late Scientific American columns, but the unusual\nL11@P0: name comes from an earlier article by Henrik Eriksson in the Swedish language\nL12@P0: journal Elementa. That article ends with a variant game which does not yet seem\nL13@P0: to have been fully analyzed. Here we provide a thorough study of Henrik Eriksson’s\nL14@P0: Bulgarian Solitaire variant and its immediate generalizations, also placing these in\nL15@P0: the context of several broad generalizations of Bulgarian Solitaire developed in the\nL16@P0: past ten years. Of particular interest, characterizing and enumerating the Garden of\nL17@P0: Eden states (those with no predecessors under the operation) uses a new “stretched”\nL18@P0: version of Dyson’s rank statistic for integer partitions.\nL19@P0: – In memory of Henrik Eriksson (1942–2017)\nL20@P0: 1. Bulgarian Solitaire\nL21@P0: Given an integer n ≥ 0, we say λ = (λ1, . . . , λs) is a partition of n if each λiis\nL22@P0: a positive integer, Pλi = n, and λ1 ≥ λ2 ≥ · · · ≥ λs (there is also one empty\nL23@P0: partition of n = 0). The number of parts s is called the length of λ. We will\nL24@P0: also use the frequency notation λ = 1m1 2\nL25@P0: m2\nL26@P0: · · · n\nL27@P0: mn which indicates that λ has m1\nL28@P0: parts 1, m2 parts 2, etc. Write P(n) for the set of partitions of n and p(n) for the\nL29@P0: number of partitions of n. (In the last section, we will expand the definition to\nL30@P0: allow partitions with certain positive rational parts.)\nL31@P0: Definition 1. Given n ≥ 1 and λ = (λ1, . . . , λs) ∈ P(n), the Bulgarian Solitaire\nL32@P0: operation B : P(n) → P(n) is determined by\nL33@P0: B(λ) = (s, λ1 − 1, . . . , λs − 1)\nL34@P0-1: DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.11352790INTEGERS: 24A (2024) 2\nL35@P1: ❤ ❤ ❤\nL36@P1: ❤ ❤\nL37@P1: ❤\nL38@P1: ❤\nL39@P1: ❤\nL40@P1: ✲\nL41@P1: ❤ ❤ ❤\nL42@P1: ❤ ❤\nL43@P1: ❤ ❤\nL44@P1: ❤\nL45@P1: Figure 1: The image of (5, 2, 1) under B is (4, 3, 1).\nL46@P1: where any zeros are removed and the parts may need to be reordered to be in\nL47@P1: nonincreasing order.\nL48@P1: This operation can be visualized using the Ferrers diagram of a partition where\nL49@P1: each part corresponds to a column of dots. A move in Bulgarian Solitaire removes\nL50@P1: one dot from each column; it is helpful to think of removing the bottom row of the\nL51@P1: diagram. See Figure 1 for an example.\nL52@P1: Applying B to all partitions of a fixed n creates a finite dynamical system; see\nL53@P1: Figure 2 showing the n = 4 case. Note that the resulting directed graph with vertices\nL54@P1: P(4) has one (weakly) connected component with a 3-cycle and one partition with\nL55@P1: no preimage.\nL56@P1: What became known as Bulgarian Solitaire dates to Moscow in 1980 and spread\nL57@P1: in mathematical circles around Europe. Henrik Eriksson heard of it from a colleague\nL58@P1: who had attended a conference in Sofia, thus his article “Bulgarisk Patiens” in\nL59@P1: the Swedish language journal Elementa [6]. He soon traveled to California and\nL60@P1: communicated the puzzle with the name Bulgarian Solitaire; from Donald Knuth\nL61@P1: and Ron Graham it passed to Martin Gardner who included it in a 1983 article\nL62@P1: [9]. See [11] for a more detailed history including Eriksson’s declaration, “The silly\nL63@P1: name is my invention, silly because it is neither Bulgarian nor a solitaire.”\nL64@P1: The compelling question of Bulgarian Solitaire concerns longterm behavior, es\u00021\nL65@P1: 4 ✲ 4 ✲ 31 ✲\nL66@P1: 211\nL67@P1: ❄\nL68@P1: 22\nL69@P1: ❅\nL70@P1: ❅\nL71@P1: ❅■\nL72@P1: Figure 2: The Bulgarian Solitaire operation B on the partitions of 4. Parentheses\nL73@P1-2: and commas are omitted, and exponents are used to denote repetition.INTEGERS: 24A (2024) 3\nL74@P2: pecially for certain numbers of objects (e.g., Gardner’s 45 playing cards). As a\nL75@P2: dynamical system on a finite set, B applied to P(n) for any n must eventually form\nL76@P2: cycles: Given λ ∈ P(n), the partitions λ, B(λ), B2(λ) = B(B(λ)), B3(λ), . . . cannot\nL77@P2: all be distinct. Call a partition λ cyclic if λ = Bj(λ) for some j. The following de\u0002scription of cyclic partitions was found independently by Bojanov [2], Eriksson [6],\nL78@P2: and Toom [17], all in problems sections of journals primarily intended for students,\nL79@P2: and by Brandt [3] in the Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society. Let\nL80@P2: Tm be the mth triangular number m(m + 1)/2.\nL81@P2: Theorem 1. Given n ≥ 1, write n uniquely as Tm + m0for some integer m0 with\nL82@P2: 0 ≤ m0 ≤ m. The cyclic partitions of n under the operation B have the form\nL83@P2: (m + rm, m − 1 + rm−1, . . . , 1 + r1, r0)\nL84@P2: where each integer ri satisfies 0 ≤ ri ≤ 1 and P\nL85@P2: i\nL86@P2: ri = m0. Therefore, there are\nL87@P2: m+1\nL88@P2: m0\nL89@P2: \u0001\nL90@P2: cyclic partitions. In particular, n has a unique cyclic partition (a 1-cycle,\nL91@P2: also known as a fixed point) when each ri = 0 and n = Tm.\nL92@P2: It is clear that the described partitions are closed under the operation B. See\nL93@P2: Drensky [4] for an expository account of the necessity part of the proof, using a\nL94@P2: “cradle” model attributed to Bj¨orner. In Figure 2, the cyclic partitions of 4 for B\nL95@P2: are (3, 1), (2, 2), and (2, 1, 1), which can be thought of as (2, 1) with an additional\nL96@P2: dot in the first, second, or third column, respectively. In general, the ri all occupy\nL97@P2: the (m + 1)st diagonal above the m full diagonals corresponding to the triangular\nL98@P2: partition (m, . . . , 1). Combinatorially, the ri correspond to a necklace of m + 1\nL99@P2: white and black beads with m0 being black, or a circular/cyclic weak composition\nL100@P2: of m0 having m + 1 parts each 0 or 1.\nL101@P2: Brandt also applied P´olya enumeration to determine the number of connected\nL102@P2: components which is the same as the number of disjoint cycles [3, Theorem 5].\nL103@P2: Theorem 2. Given n ≥ 1, write n uniquely as Tm + m0for some integer m0 with\nL104@P2: 0 ≤ m0 ≤ m. The number of connected components in P(n) under the operation B\nL105@P2: is\nL106@P2: 1\nL107@P2: m + 1\nL108@P2: X\nL109@P2: d|(m+1,m0)\nL110@P2: ϕ(d)\nL111@P2: \u0012\nL112@P2: (m + 1)/d\nL113@P2: m0/d \u0013\nL114@P2: where (m + 1, m0) denotes the greatest common divisor of m + 1 and m0, and ϕ(d)\nL115@P2: is the Euler phi function.\nL116@P2: The smallest n for which Bulgarian Solitaire disconnects P(n) into multiple com\u0002ponents is n = 8. However, there is a single connected components only when n is\nL117@P2: within one of a triangular number.\nL118@P2: Other distinguished partitions in these dynamical systems are those without a\nL119@P2: preimage, such as (1, 1, 1, 1) in Figure 2. These are known as Garden of Eden (GE)\nL120@P2-3: partitions.INTEGERS: 24A (2024) 4\nL121@P3: The most succinct characterization of GE partitions uses a partition statistic\nL122@P3: defined by Dyson [5]: The rank of λ = (λ1, . . . , λs) is λ1 − s. For instance,\nL123@P3: rank((5, 2, 1)) = 5 − 3 = 2.\nL124@P3: Write N(m, n) for the number of partitions λ of n with rank(λ) = m. We will\nL125@P3: need the following two generating functions, given with standard q-series notation\nL126@P3: such as the q-Pochhammer symbol (a; q)n =\nL127@P3: Qn−1\nL128@P3: i=0 (1 − aqi\nL129@P3: ) for n > 0.\nL130@P3: Proposition 3. Given an integer m, two generating functions for N(m, n) are\nL131@P3: X\nL132@P3: n≥0\nL133@P3: N(m, n)q\nL134@P3: n =\nL135@P3: 1\nL136@P3: (q; q)∞\nL137@P3: X\nL138@P3: n≥1\nL139@P3: (−1)n−1q\nL140@P3: 1\nL141@P3: 2 n(3n−1)+mn(1 − q\nL142@P3: n\nL143@P3: ), (1)\nL144@P3: X\nL145@P3: n≥0\nL146@P3: X\nL147@P3: m∈Z\nL148@P3: N(m, n)w\nL149@P3: mqn =\nL150@P3: X\nL151@P3: n≥0\nL152@P3: q\nL153@P3: n\nL154@P3: 2\nL155@P3: (wq; q)n(w−1q; q)n\nL156@P3: . (2)\nL157@P3: Proof. Equation (1) was stated by Dyson [5] and proven by Atkin and Swinnerton\u0002Dyer [1].\nL158@P3: Equation (2) makes use of the Durfee square of a partition, the largest possible\nL159@P3: square starting from the lower left corner of the Ferrers diagram (as we draw them\nL160@P3: here). The subpartition above an n × n Durfee square has at most n parts and the\nL161@P3: subpartition to the right of the Durfee square has largest part at most n which gives\nL162@P3: Jacobi’s result\nL163@P3: X\nL164@P3: n≥0\nL165@P3: p(n)q\nL166@P3: n =\nL167@P3: X\nL168@P3: n≥0\nL169@P3: q\nL170@P3: n\nL171@P3: 2\nL172@P3: (q; q)\nL173@P3: 2\nL174@P3: n\nL175@P3: .\nL176@P3: Inside the Durfee square, there are an equal number of dots in the left column and\nL177@P3: in the bottom row, so they are not necessary for the rank computation. In order\nL178@P3: for the exponent of w to record the rank, the dots above the Durfee square in the\nL179@P3: left column each have weight w while the dots to the right of the Durfee square in\nL180@P3: the bottom row each have weight w\nL181@P3: −1\nL182@P3: .\nL183@P3: See Figure 3 for the partition (5, 2, 1), its Durfee square size 2×2, and the weights\nL184@P3: w and w\nL185@P3: −1\nL186@P3: contributing to its rank, 2.\nL187@P3: The following characterization of Garden of Eden partitions was given by the\nL188@P3: author and Jones [12] soon followed by the description using rank and enumeration\nL189@P3: results by the author and Sellers [14]. Write ge(n) for the number of GE partitions\nL190@P3: of n under B.\nL191@P3: Theorem 4. The Garden of Eden partitions of n under B are exactly the λ ∈ P(n)\nL192@P3: for which rank(λ) ≤ −2.\nL193@P3: A generating function for ge(n) is\nL194@P3: X\nL195@P3: n≥0\nL196@P3: ge(n)q\nL197@P3: n =\nL198@P3: X\nL199@P3: m≤−2\nL200@P3: 1\nL201@P3: (q; q)∞\nL202@P3: X\nL203@P3: r≥1\nL204@P3: (−1)r−1\nL205@P3: \u0010\nL206@P3: q\nL207@P3: (3r\nL208@P3: 2−r)/2 + q\nL209@P3: (3r\nL210@P3: 2+r)/2\nL211@P3: \u0011\nL212@P3: q\nL213@P3: |m|r\nL214@P3-4: (3)INTEGERS: 24A (2024) 5\nL215@P4: ✍✌\nL216@P4: ✎☞\nL217@P4: ✍✌\nL218@P4: ✎☞\nL219@P4: ✍✌\nL220@P4: ✎☞w\nL221@P4: ✍✌\nL222@P4: ✎☞w\n"
